# phext-dep-lint — audit record home

This phext is the policy source of truth for phext-minimum-viable-
dependencies. One scroll per crate (volumes 2.. of this book) records
an audit: what the crate is, its own transitive tree, why it is
needed, and a verdict. A crate may be used only when its audit scroll
exists here with `verdict: pass`. No scroll = no audit on record =
denied, including crates existing code already uses — those are
findings, not grandfathered rights.

Will's rule (2026-11-26): "run all rust deps through a phext-based
audit — if I haven't approached a library, it doesn't get used."

transitive-budget: 60

Budget reasoning (seed, EE 1017): measured locks of the de-slopped
lineage — phext-serve 29 packages, holo-phext 23, SQ 55. A budget of
60 admits the lineage's own proven shapes and catches the slop case
(phext-lattice at 218 with its default web feature) by a wide margin.
Per-project budget changes happen only as recorded exceptions in
policy/exceptions.toml.

Scroll format (fields the linter reads are `crate:` and `verdict:`;
the rest is the audit record humans read):
  crate / examined-version / what / own-tree / needed-for /
  verdict (pass|fail) / reasoning / audited-by + date + EE.
crate: libphext
examined-version: 0.6.6 (lock resolution; manifests request 0.6.4)
what: The phext format library itself — the substrate every other
  piece of the lineage is built on. First-party (wbic16), not an
  external dependency in any meaningful sense.
own-tree: 3 direct externals of its own — xxhash-rust (mandatory),
  base64 (optional, `binary` feature), xz2 (optional, `xz` feature).
  Each is audited separately in this phext.
needed-for: Everything. phext-lattice, SQ, phext-serve, holo-phext
  all sit on it.
verdict: pass
reasoning: First-party substrate; refusing it would refuse the
  format. Its own external surface is minimal and each external is
  individually audited (xxhash-rust pass, base64 pass, xz2 FAIL —
  the `xz` feature is enabled by no consumer and should stay off).
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: holo-phext
examined-version: git rev bafed5f (locks pin a rev described as
  0.2.5; the repo's own manifest says 0.2.0 — version drift on
  record, to reconcile in the holo-phext repo)
what: The layer-2 holographic store engine over libphext — the
  transaction/index layer phext-lattice and phext-serve host.
  First-party (wbic16).
own-tree: libphext (git), memmap2, tokio — each audited separately.
  Its own lock resolves to 23 packages total.
needed-for: phext-lattice's store, phext-serve's hosting. The
  Variant C verdict (2026-11-26): holo-phext survives as the
  transaction layer inside the lattice design line.
verdict: pass
reasoning: First-party engine with a small, fully-audited external
  surface (23-package lock). The version drift noted above is a
  bookkeeping defect in the holo-phext repo, not a dependency
  problem; it is recorded here so it is not silent.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: xxhash-rust
examined-version: 0.8.5 (features: xxh3)
what: Rust implementation of xxHash (xxh3), the content hash
  libphext uses for addressing and integrity.
own-tree: LEAF. Zero dependencies of its own.
needed-for: libphext core hashing — not optional in practice; the
  format's addressing depends on a fast stable content hash and
  std provides none.
verdict: pass
reasoning: The cleanest shape an external crate can have: a leaf,
  single-purpose, doing one thing the standard library does not
  do. This is what minimum-viable looks like.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: base64
examined-version: 0.22
what: Base64 encoding/decoding (RFC 4648).
own-tree: LEAF. Zero dependencies of its own.
needed-for: libphext's optional `binary` feature — binary payloads
  carried in phext form. Enabled by phext-lattice's use of libphext
  [binary].
verdict: pass
reasoning: Leaf, single-purpose, and std has no base64. The cost
  of the dependency is exactly one crate; the cost of hand-rolling
  a correct codec is a permanent correctness surface. Pass.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: percent-encoding
examined-version: 2.3.1
what: Percent-encoding/decoding for URLs (RFC 3986).
own-tree: LEAF. Zero dependencies of its own.
needed-for: SQ's URL decoding on its serving path.
verdict: pass
reasoning: A leaf. Strictly, percent-decoding is small enough to
  hand-roll — but a zero-drag, single-purpose, spec-exact crate
  costs nothing transitively, and the audit standard is minimum
  viable dependencies, not minimum possible crate count at any
  correctness risk. Pass, recorded as convenience-with-zero-drag.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: memmap2
examined-version: 0.9
what: Cross-platform memory-mapped file access — the mechanism
  the lattice store is built on (a phext file is mmapped, not read).
own-tree: Near-leaf: 1 dependency of its own (libc on unix).
needed-for: phext-lattice core storage and holo-phext. This is
  not a convenience; mmap IS the storage mechanism of the design.
verdict: pass
reasoning: Essential mechanism, one-package drag, and the safe
  wrapper over mmap syscalls is precisely the kind of unsafe-
  adjacent surface a small audited crate should own instead of
  every consumer hand-rolling it. Pass.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: rayon
examined-version: 1.10
what: Work-stealing parallelism (parallel iterators) for CPU-bound
  batch work.
own-tree: Small tree: 2 direct deps of its own, 5 packages
  reachable (rayon-core, crossbeam-deque/epoch/queue/utils class).
needed-for: phext-lattice's parallel search and index paths — a
  measured performance surface of the lattice core, not decoration.
verdict: pass
reasoning: Bounded, known tree (5 packages), all of them small
  concurrency primitives, used on a real hot path. The alternative
  is hand-rolled thread pools in the lattice — a worse correctness
  surface than this drag. Pass; if the tree grows materially the
  audit is re-opened.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: serde
examined-version: 1.0 (with derive)
what: The standard Rust serialization framework; SQ uses it with
  derive for its wire/state structures. (In phext-lattice, serde
  appears only behind the `web` feature — not core there.)
own-tree: 2 direct (serde_core, serde_derive), 6 reachable; the
  derive path is proc-macro weight (proc-macro2, quote, syn) at
  build time.
needed-for: SQ core. SQ's structures serialize through serde;
  there is no std serialization framework to fall back to.
verdict: pass
reasoning: Examined and passed with its cost named: the tree is
  small (6) but the derive feature imports the proc-macro stack
  into every build. That is the accepted price for the estate's
  one serialization framework — the alternative (a hand-rolled
  serializer per project) is exactly the divergence slop this
  policy exists to prevent. New uses should prefer serde without
  derive where practical.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: serde_json
examined-version: 1.0
what: JSON serialization/deserialization on serde — the REST wire
  format for SQ and phext-serve.
own-tree: 5 direct deps of its own, 10 reachable in the smallest
  consuming lock. Watch item: current locks show a new crate,
  zmij, occupying the slot ryu used to hold (float formatting) —
  it entered transitively without an audit and is exactly the
  kind of silent tree change this phext exists to catch; it
  stands as an unaudited-transitive finding until audited.
needed-for: SQ and phext-serve HTTP surfaces. JSON is the wire
  format other tools already speak to them.
verdict: pass
reasoning: The wire format is a real requirement and 10 known
  packages is a bounded, visible cost. Passed for the crate
  itself; its own dependents do not inherit this pass — each is
  audited or flagged on its own (see the zmij watch item).
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: tokio
examined-version: 1 (features in use: rt-multi-thread, macros,
  net, io-util, sync, time, signal)
what: The async runtime — executor, reactor, timers, async IO —
  under phext-serve's HTTP hosting and holo-phext's engine.
own-tree: The heaviest legitimate tree in the core: 2 direct deps
  at minimal features, 6–15 reachable depending on the feature set
  (mio, socket2, parking_lot, pin-project-lite class packages).
needed-for: phext-serve (async HTTP server) and holo-phext. An
  async server in Rust without a runtime means hand-building an
  executor/reactor — not a serious option, and pretending
  otherwise is how estates rot.
verdict: pass
reasoning: Passed WITH feature discipline, which is where tokio's
  real risk lives: the approved surface is the feature set named
  above, the set the lineage actually uses. Enabling new tokio
  features (fs, process, full, …) widens the tree silently and
  requires re-audit before use. The runtime itself is essential,
  maintained, and bounded under that discipline.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: tempfile
examined-version: 3
what: Temporary file/directory creation with automatic cleanup.
own-tree: 5 direct deps of its own, 12 packages reachable —
  including rustix and windows-sys and an RNG stack (getrandom/
  rand) — to create a temporary file.
needed-for: Claimed by phext-lattice. Nothing in the lattice's
  design requires OS temp files as a mechanism; the uses are
  conveniences of the "just grab tempfile" kind.
verdict: fail
reasoning: The canonical minimum-viable violation: twelve
  packages, a Windows syscall binding, and a randomness stack,
  imported to do what std does with temp_dir() plus a unique
  name — a few lines, owned, auditable. A crate whose entire
  job is trivially std-shaped and whose tree is not fails this
  audit. phext-lattice's use stands as a baseline finding until
  the uses are replaced or a recorded per-project exception
  argues otherwise.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: xz2
examined-version: 0.1
what: Rust bindings to the C liblzma (xz compression), with a
  build script that compiles/links C.
own-tree: Tree (xz-sys + cc build machinery). Present in NO
  consumer's lock: libphext declares it behind the optional `xz`
  feature and no project in the fleet enables that feature.
needed-for: Nothing currently running. It is a speculative
  capability carried in a manifest.
verdict: fail
reasoning: An unused C binding with a build script is pure
  attack/churn surface: it costs audit attention, build time,
  and a native dependency on the ranch's offline route, in
  exchange for a feature nobody has switched on. If a real
  consumer for xz-compressed phext appears, it can be
  re-audited then — with the consumer named. Until then: fail.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: raw_sync
examined-version: 0.1.5
what: Raw OS synchronization primitives (mutexes, events) over
  shared memory, for inter-process coordination.
own-tree: 5 direct deps of its own, 14 packages reachable.
needed-for: SQ's IPC path — SQ coordinates processes through
  shared memory and uses raw_sync's primitives there. It is on
  SQ's critical path, not an edge convenience.
verdict: fail
reasoning: A 0.1.x, slow-moving crate carrying a 14-package tree
  on the critical path of the estate's live-edit substrate is
  the wrong shape: the primitives it provides are a thin layer
  over OS facilities SQ could own directly, and its maintenance
  posture means the estate effectively owns the risk already
  without owning the code. FAIL as the audit verdict. SQ's
  continued use is recorded as a per-project exception in
  policy/exceptions.toml — visible, reasoned, and the first
  candidate for a first-party replacement — not silently
  grandfathered.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
crate: shared_memory
examined-version: 0.12.4
what: Cross-platform shared-memory allocation/mapping for IPC.
own-tree: 5 direct deps of its own, 18 packages reachable — the
  largest single drag in SQ's tree (SQ's whole lock is 55).
needed-for: SQ's IPC architecture: SQ's processes coordinate
  through shared memory; this crate is its foundation.
verdict: fail
reasoning: Eighteen packages to map shared memory — a facility
  the OS provides and the lineage already wraps elsewhere at
  near-leaf cost (memmap2: 1). Auditing shared_memory honestly
  means auditing SQ's IPC architecture, and the verdict is that
  the architecture's dependency shape is not minimum-viable
  even though SQ itself is essential and shipping. FAIL as the
  audit verdict. SQ's continued use is recorded as a per-project
  exception in policy/exceptions.toml with the migration
  question left open and visible — not a silent pass.
audited-by: Emise, phext-dep-lint seed pass, 2026-11-26, EE 1017.
